    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Position Vector of a Particle Moving With Constant Velocity",
    "problem": "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(i-4j)\\,\\text{km}$ with a constant velocity of $i\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $2$ hours.",
    "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$  the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=i-4j,\\,u=i.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=2\\,\\text{h}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(i-4j)+i(2) \\\\  & =(i-4j)+2i \\\\  & =(3i-4j)\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
