    "reasoning": "Each selected answer agrees with its own captured worked solution. q-136375: r(2)=(i-4j)+2i=3i-4j. q-136416: r(2)=-2j+2(3i+j)+(1/2)(4i)(4)=14i. q-136437: r(6)=(2i-4j)+6(3i-j)+18(-i+j)=2i+8j, whose magnitude is 2\\sqrt{17}. q-24575: using cos(2t)=2cos²(t)-1 gives y=4(2x²/9-1); the parametrization additionally restricts x to [-3,3], an inherited domain restriction omitted from the listed relation. q-87135: the fourth-quadrant ray has reference angle \\pi/6, so its angle is 11\\pi/6. q-120719: (x,y)=(12cos(7\\pi/6),12sin(7\\pi/6))=(-6\\sqrt{3},-6). q-115348: L(x)=5+x/\\sqrt{2}, hence L(\\sqrt{2})=6; concavity down places the tangent above the graph. q-121715: componentwise differentiation and the chain rule give v=⟨-6t,2cos(2t+1)⟩. q-7151: v(4)=⟨4,3⟩ has magnitude 5. q-121738: both velocity components vanish only at their shared root t=1. q-28219: conjugate roots yield 2[(x-2)²+1]=2x²-8x+10=0. q-160008: conjugate roots yield 2[(x-2)²+3]=2x²-8x+14=0. q-107551: multiplying the sine and cosine values by \\sqrt{2} gives -1+i. q-20414: the depicted intersection B is at -\\pi/6+\\pi=5\\pi/6. q-121127: arctan(8)≈82.875°, with the second solution 262.875°, rounding to 83° and 263°. q-87837: the inequality requires (x+7)²≤-5, impossible for real x. No submitted-grade evidence or earlier answer is supplied; all MA attribution below comes from captured worked solutions.",
